# Every layer that denies having a remainder, beside whether its own two figures give one. One row
# per denial, `violates` where the layer states both a demand and a nameplate, so the remainder it
# says does not exist can be worked out from the filing itself, even where the two coincide.
#
# A denial its own figures refute
#   The rule: a layer that denies having a remainder is not contradicted by its own figures. If a
#   layer files a demand and a nameplate, the nameplate less the demand is a quantity the document
#   itself supplies, and the page the denial is written on refutes it. This spans three elements
#   of one layer, which is why no grammar can state it.
#
# No way out for an exact coincidence
#   "The two coincide exactly" does not belong in the condition. A single nameplate value equal to
#   a single demand value is a remainder of zero, and `Fit`'s annotation settles that case: it is
#   a clearance fit, carrying ISO 286's answer for zero minimum clearance over to the one pair its
#   clauses do not separate. A clearance is a remainder, filed with a sign and a quantity of zero
#   at all three points. Exempting it would let the missing element stand in for a filing the
#   schema has already decided, which is the last place `none` on a remainder could hide.
#
# A computed remainder is filed, not denied
#   A layer whose remainder the receiver computes files the remainder, and names `magnitude` as
#   what computes its quantity. The denial takes a `pm:ClaimAbsence`, `unmeasured` or
#   `notApplicable`, and a stated pair contradicts either.
#
# Why the rule is not about holders
#   "A denied remainder names no holders and no absorber" could never fail: `Holder` and
#   `absorber` live inside the `Remainder` element, so a layer that declined the element cannot
#   carry either. The schema already enforces it by its structure, and the absorber half is a
#   CHECK in the DDL. A rule that cannot fail examines nothing, however many rows it counts.
#
# Thin, but it examines something
#   On the corpus both denials are on layers that state no nameplate, a ratio and an object store,
#   so there is nothing for a demand to be subtracted from and both pass. The rule examines real
#   rows and could fail on the next filing.
-- pm:StatedRemainder's absent branch against the layer's own pm:Demand and pm:Nameplate, from
-- layers/figures.sqlc.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT d.filing, d.layer,
           (f.d_low IS NOT NULL AND f.n_low IS NOT NULL)     AS violates,
           CASE WHEN f.d_low IS NULL OR f.n_low IS NULL
                THEN format('`%s`, and no remainder is derivable: %s',
                            d.reason,
                            CASE WHEN f.d_low IS NULL THEN 'no demand is stated'
                                 ELSE 'no nameplate is stated' END)
                ELSE format('`%s`, yet demand [%s, %s] against a nameplate of [%s, %s] '
                            'gives a remainder the filing supplies itself',
                            d.reason, f.d_low, f.d_high, f.n_low, f.n_high)
           END AS detail
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/denied_remainders.sqlc)
    ) d
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(layers/figures.sqlc)
    ) f USING (filing, layer)
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'denied_remainder_is_not_contradicted'
